Web App Coursework:

Prerequisites:
Java 25 
Maven 3.9+

Project Structure:

- data: Contains the csv data files
- src/java: Contains the model and servlets; it also includes the main file  
            acts as the entry point of the project.
- src/webapp: All jsp and html file, organized based on what each roughly does
- target and war-file both created by Maven

Compile:

Use to create War-file:
mvn clean package

Run:

Use the command:
mvn clean compile exec:exec

It opens on the default port:
http://localhost:8080

Configuration:

Port configuration can be changed by running:
SERVER_PORT=your-port mvn clean compile exec:exec

Usage:

The web app is quite minimalistic; focused mostly on functionality rather than aesthetics.
If there is no error when reading the files; when someone accesses the link they will be 
greeted with a variety of option. Navigating them is relatively easy and there is always the option 
at the bottom of the page to go back on the main page(Except for the graph pages). The options 
available are pretty self explanatory but I have included a small description for some below.

IMPORTANT
To delete or modify a patient you must first navigate to their personal page. This is done
either through the "Search with a keyword" or "Find Patient Id" both of whom have
esentially the same function; although find patient id is a bit more finicky.

"Find patient id" - Find a patients Id and personal page with their name
"Search with keyword" - Same thing as the previous one just that it will comb
                        through all the data. ie. It can give some pretty 
                        irrelevant answers.
"Find how many patients there are" - counts dead patients too.
"Export all patients as JSON" - the json is going to appear the main directory of the project.

When it comes to the data used I added all the patient csv but I use the 1000 one
or if available the modified version made by the user. All of them could be 
used by changing a line in the DataLoader. But personally I think that 1000
looks the best for the graphs.